\section{The log prior term}{

The term, attribute \code{log_prior} of \code{logLik_penalized()}, is exactly
what the optimizer adds. It is the sum of each prior's log density on
the scale that the optimizer moves, so it includes the log-Jacobian
of every map from that scale to the parameter the prior is about: the
log of a standard deviation (\code{sd}, \code{sigma} and other positive
parameters), a dpar's link, the chart of an autocorrelation
coefficient, the increments of ordinal thresholds, the chart of a
simplex (\code{simo} and other Dirichlet priors) and the Cholesky chart of
an \code{lkj()} prior. A prior with a bound has no truncation normalizer:
the bound is a box on the optimizer, not a change in the density.

So the term is not brms's \code{lprior}. brms evaluates each density on the
constrained scale with no Jacobian, which Stan adds to \code{lp__}
separately, and divides a bounded prior by the probability inside its
bounds. For \code{normal(0, 1)} on an \code{sd} at the value \code{s}, the term here
is \code{dnorm(s, 0, 1, log = TRUE) + log(s)}; brms's \code{lprior} is
\code{dnorm(s, 0, 1, log = TRUE) + log(2)}. Only the term here is what
separates \code{logLik()} from \code{logLik_penalized()}.
