\section{Random effects and REML}{

With random effects, the objective is an integral over them: the
Laplace approximation, \code{quadrature = TRUE} or \verb{importance =}. A prior
on a parameter outside the integral (a fixed effect, a standard
deviation, a correlation) is a constant in that integral, so the
split is exact. Under \code{REML = TRUE} the fixed effects are inside the
integral, so a prior on them (class \code{b}, \code{Intercept}, or an \code{nlpar})
does not separate from it. Then \code{logLik()} stops with an error, and
\code{logLik_penalized()} still gives the maximized value.
}

\section{Information criteria}{

The MAP estimates do not maximize the log-likelihood, and a parameter
that a prior shrinks is not a full degree of freedom. Thus, under a
prior with a density, \code{logLik()} has \code{df = NA}, with the reason in
attribute \code{df_why}. \code{AIC()}, \code{BIC()}, \code{extractAIC()} and so \code{step()}
