\code{AIC(lm_fit, map_fit)}, \code{BIC(lm_fit, map_fit)} or
\code{AIC(logLik(map_fit))}, gets \code{NA} for that fit. A package that counts
the parameters itself is not covered: \code{insight::get_loglikelihood()}
replaces the \code{NA} with its own count, so \code{performance} can still give
an AIC for such a fit. Refit without the prior to compare models by
AIC.

A prior that only sets bounds (\code{set_prior("", class = "sd", lb = 0.1)}) adds no density, and these methods work. So does a \code{uniform()}
prior on a parameter that the optimizer moves on its own scale (a
